---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import '../../styles/packmetry-workspace.css';
import '../../styles/methodology.css';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Packing Algorithm & Verification Methodology — Packmetry"
  description="How Packmetry generates deterministic packing candidates, independently verifies geometry and constraints, ranks valid options and constructs the canonical plan shown in 3D."
>
  <main class="pm-methodology-article-page">
    <div class="pm-methodology-article-shell">
      <header class="pm-methodology-article-hero">
        <p class="pm-methodology-kicker">
          Packing algorithm &amp; verification
        </p>

        <h1>
          From solver candidate
          to verified packing plan.
        </h1>

        <p>
          Packmetry is built around a separation
          of responsibilities: the solver proposes
          arrangements, an independent verifier
          checks them, objective logic ranks only
          verified candidates, and the canonical
          plan becomes the source of truth for
          metrics and 3D presentation.
        </p>
      </header>

      <section
        class="pm-methodology-flow"
        aria-label="Packmetry planning pipeline"
      >
        <div class="pm-methodology-flow-grid">
          <article class="pm-methodology-flow-step">
            <span>01</span>
            <h2>Normalize inputs</h2>
            <p>
              Item, carton, unit, constraint and
              objective data enter the shared
              domain model.
            </p>
          </article>

          <article class="pm-methodology-flow-step">
            <span>02</span>
            <h2>Generate candidates</h2>
            <p>
              The deterministic baseline solver
              creates a small bounded set of
              heuristic packing candidates.
            </p>
          </article>

          <article class="pm-methodology-flow-step">
            <span>03</span>
            <h2>Verify independently</h2>
            <p>
              Every candidate is checked by a
              separate verification boundary before
              it is eligible for recommendation.
            </p>
          </article>

          <article class="pm-methodology-flow-step">
            <span>04</span>
            <h2>Rank valid options</h2>
            <p>
              Packing coverage is protected first,
              then the selected objective ranks
              comparable verified candidates.
            </p>
          </article>

          <article class="pm-methodology-flow-step">
            <span>05</span>
            <h2>Construct canonical plan</h2>
            <p>
              One selected verified candidate is
              converted into the canonical
              PackingPlan and canonical metrics.
            </p>
          </article>

          <article class="pm-methodology-flow-step">
            <span>06</span>
            <h2>Present the result</h2>
            <p>
              Result summaries and the 3D view
              consume the canonical verified plan
              instead of rebuilding placement logic.
            </p>
          </article>
        </div>
      </section>

      <div class="pm-methodology-content">
        <article class="pm-methodology-prose">
          <section>
            <p class="pm-methodology-kicker">
              Candidate generation
            </p>

            <h2>
              The current baseline is deterministic,
              bounded and heuristic.
            </h2>

            <p>
              Packmetry&apos;s current production
              solver is the deterministic
              <strong>packmetry-baseline</strong>
              solver. It processes item instances
              in input order, tries already-open
              carton instances before opening new
              ones, and uses deterministic
              axis-aligned candidate points and
              allowed rotation order.
            </p>

            <p>
              For the same normalized input, the
              solver evaluates carton types using
              up to three order strategies:
            </p>

            <ol>
              <li>
                carton types in the original input
                order;
              </li>
              <li>
                smallest internal carton volume
                first;
              </li>
              <li>
                largest internal carton volume
                first.
              </li>
            </ol>

            <p>
              Exact duplicate candidate plans are
              removed while preserving the first
              occurrence. The resulting candidate
              set therefore contains between one
              and three candidates.
            </p>

            <div class="pm-methodology-callout">
              <strong>Important limitation</strong>
              <p>
                This is a bounded heuristic search,
                not exhaustive 3D bin-packing
                enumeration. Packmetry describes
                the result as a recommended or best
                plan found, not a guaranteed global
                mathematical optimum.
              </p>
            </div>
          </section>

          <section>
            <p class="pm-methodology-kicker">
              Independent verification
            </p>

            <h2>
              Solver output is treated as an
              untrusted claim.
            </h2>

            <p>
              A candidate does not become trusted
              merely because Packmetry generated it.
              Each candidate passes through a
              separate verifier before ranking or
              canonical-plan construction.
            </p>

            <p>
              The current verifier checks, among
              other invariants:
            </p>

            <ul>
              <li>
                referenced item and carton IDs
                exist;
              </li>
              <li>
                item instance indexes are valid and
                item instances are counted once;
              </li>
              <li>
                placement dimensions match the
                declared rotation and rotation
                policy;
              </li>
              <li>
                placements stay inside carton
                internal boundaries;
              </li>
              <li>
                placements do not overlap;
              </li>
              <li>
                known gross-weight limits are not
                exceeded;
              </li>
              <li>
                carton inventory limits are not
                overused.
              </li>
            </ul>

            <p>
              Verification failures remain
              observable. The planning pipeline
              does not silently repair an invalid
              candidate and present the repaired
              version as if the solver had produced
              a valid result.
            </p>
          </section>

          <section>
            <p class="pm-methodology-kicker">
              Recommendation logic
            </p>

            <h2>
              Coverage comes before optimization.
            </h2>

            <p>
              Only independently verified
              candidates are eligible for ranking.
              Before an optimization objective is
              applied, Packmetry first prefers the
              candidates that place the greatest
              number of requested item instances.
            </p>

            <p>
              This prevents an objective such as
              fewer cartons from winning simply
              because an incomplete candidate used
              fewer boxes. Only candidates tied at
              the best packing-coverage level move
              on to objective-specific comparison.
            </p>

            <p>
              Current product objectives can then
              compare verified candidates using
              deterministic metrics such as carton
              count, wasted internal volume, known
              carton cost, known gross-weight data
              or dimensional weight where the
              required data is available.
            </p>
          </section>

          <section>
            <p class="pm-methodology-kicker">
              Canonical result
            </p>

            <h2>
              The selected verified candidate is
              converted into one canonical plan.
            </h2>

            <p>
              Canonical plan construction derives
              final carton contents, placements,
              status and metrics from the selected
              verified candidate. Canonical status
              is derived from verified item
              accounting rather than blindly copied
              from a solver status field.
            </p>

            <p>
              Utilization and empty-volume metrics
              use carton internal dimensions,
              because those dimensions describe the
              usable packing cavity. Known physical
              weight metrics remain separate and
              unknown values are not silently
              treated as zero.
            </p>
          </section>

          <section>
            <p class="pm-methodology-kicker">
              3D presentation
            </p>

            <h2>
              The visualization follows the
              canonical plan.
            </h2>

            <p>
              Packmetry&apos;s 3D view is a
              comprehension layer, not another
              packing engine. It receives canonical
              verified placements and renders those
              coordinates. It does not independently
              invent a more attractive arrangement.
            </p>

            <p>
              This keeps the textual result,
              metrics and 3D arrangement attached
              to the same source of truth.
            </p>
          </section>

          <section>
            <p class="pm-methodology-kicker">
              DIM and carrier boundary
            </p>

            <h2>
              Dimensional weight does not change
              packing geometry.
            </h2>

            <p>
              Physical packing uses carton internal
              dimensions. When dimensional-weight
              analysis is enabled, DIM calculations
              use external package dimensions and
              an explicit unit-aware divisor.
            </p>

            <p>
              DIM settings can participate in
              verified-candidate ranking and
              canonical metrics, but the baseline
              solver does not move items differently
              because a DIM divisor was supplied.
              Packmetry also does not turn that DIM
              result into live carrier pricing,
              service-specific billing rounding,
              zones or shipping-rate quotes.
            </p>

            <div class="pm-methodology-callout">
              <strong>Current constraint boundary</strong>
              <p>
                Fragile handling, padding, spacing
                and stackability remain outside the
                currently enforced solver/verifier
                semantics where the product cannot
                yet guarantee those behaviors.
              </p>
            </div>
          </section>
        </article>

        <aside class="pm-methodology-aside">
          <p class="pm-methodology-kicker">
            Current baseline
          </p>

          <strong>
            What Packmetry can truthfully claim
          </strong>

          <ul>
            <li>
              deterministic bounded candidate
              generation;
            </li>
            <li>
              independent verification before
              recommendation;
            </li>
            <li>
              objective-aware ranking of verified
              candidates;
            </li>
            <li>
              canonical result metrics and
              placements;
            </li>
            <li>
              3D rendering from canonical verified
              placements;
            </li>
            <li>
              no claim of guaranteed global
              optimality.
            </li>
          </ul>

          <div class="pm-methodology-links">
            <a href="/personal/">
              Try the Personal workspace →
            </a>

            <a href="/business/">
              Open the Business workspace →
            </a>

            <a href="/tools/dimensional-weight-calculator/">
              Use the DIM calculator →
            </a>

            <a href="/guides/dimensional-weight/">
              Read the DIM guide →
            </a>
          </div>
        </aside>
      </div>

      <section class="pm-methodology-cta">
        <div>
          <p class="pm-methodology-kicker">
            Put the methodology to work
          </p>

          <h2>
            Run a real packing problem through
            the same verified pipeline.
          </h2>

          <p>
            Personal keeps the workflow simple.
            Business exposes more operational data
            such as carton inventory, costs,
            weights and DIM settings while using
            the same core planning architecture.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="pm-methodology-cta-actions">
          <a href="/personal/">
            Personal workspace
          </a>

          <a
            class="pm-methodology-secondary-link"
            href="/business/"
          >
            Business workspace
          </a>
        </div>
      </section>
    </div>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
